{"name":"Sailor’s Knot","hex":"#b8a47a","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sailor","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person who sails; one whose occupation is sailing or navigating ships or other waterborne craft.","In particular, a member of the crew of a (civilian or military) vessel, as opposed to a captain, admiral, etc.; a mariner; a common seaman."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sailor"},{"term":"knot","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A looping of a piece of string or of any other long, flexible material that cannot be untangled without passing one or both ends of the material through its loops.","A tangled clump of hair or similar."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form into a knot; to tie with a knot or knots.","To form wrinkles in the forehead, as a sign of concentration, concern, surprise, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/knot"}],"entries":[{"title":"Carrick bend","blocks":[{"text":"The Carrick bend, also known as the Sailor's breastplate, is a knot used for joining two lines. It is particularly appropriate for very heavy rope or cable that is too large and stiff to be easily formed into other common bends. It will not jam even after carrying a significant load or being soaked with water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Knife-lanyard-knot-ABOK-787-Carrick-start.jpg/330px-Knife-lanyard-knot-ABOK-787-Carrick-start.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carrick_bend","wikidata":"Q2142794"},{"title":"Knot","blocks":[{"text":"A knot is an intentional complication in cordage which may be practical or decorative, or both. Practical knots are classified by function, including hitches, bends, loop knots, and splices: a hitch fastens a rope to another object; a bend fastens two ends of a rope to each other; a loop knot is any knot creating a loop; and splice denotes any multi-strand knot, including bends and loops. A knot may also refer, in the strictest sense, to a stopper or knob at the end of a rope to keep that end from slipping through a grommet or eye. Knots have excited interest since ancient times for their practical uses, as well as their topological intricacy, studied in the area of topology known as knot theory."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Nf_knots.png/330px-Nf_knots.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knot","wikidata":"Q527395"},{"title":"Sailor","blocks":[{"text":"A sailor, seaman, mariner, or seafarer is a person who works aboard a watercraft as part of its crew, and may work in any one of a number of different fields that are related to the operation and maintenance of a ship. While the term sailor has its etymological roots from sailing, that is a time when sailing ships were the main mode of transport at sea, it now refers to the personnel of all watercraft regardless of the type of vessel, boat or ship. It encompasses people who operate ships professionally, be it for a military (navy) or civilian or for sports or recreation. In a navy, there may be further distinctions: sailor may refer to any member of the navy even if they are based on land, while seaman may refer to a specific enlisted rank. Additionally, fisherman are seen as a distinct type of sailor, that is those engaged in fishing."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Kristina_Regina_wheelhouse.jpg/330px-Kristina_Regina_wheelhouse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sailor","wikidata":"Q45199"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Carrick-bend-ABOK-1439-Capsized.jpg/500px-Carrick-bend-ABOK-1439-Capsized.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Carrick-bend-ABOK-1439-Capsized.jpg/1280px-Carrick-bend-ABOK-1439-Capsized.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Carrick-bend-ABOK-1439-Capsized.jpg/500px-Carrick-bend-ABOK-1439-Capsized.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACarrick-bend-ABOK-1439-Capsized.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/96/Complete_Guide_to_Heraldry_Fig685.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/96/Complete_Guide_to_Heraldry_Fig685.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Wake or Ormonde knot of heraldry","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AComplete_Guide_to_Heraldry_Fig685.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/CarrickBendCapsizingSmall.gif/330px-CarrickBendCapsizingSmall.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/CarrickBendCapsizingSmall.gif/330px-CarrickBendCapsizingSmall.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Complete capsizing requires a loose weave.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACarrickBendCapsizingSmall.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Carrick-bend-seized-ABOK-1439.jpg/500px-Carrick-bend-seized-ABOK-1439.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Carrick-bend-seized-ABOK-1439.jpg/1280px-Carrick-bend-seized-ABOK-1439.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/